  { q: "What are stress factors in Mifflin-St Jeor?", a: "Stress factors — also called injury or illness factors — increase your calorie needs above normal BMR because your body works harder to heal, fight infection, or cope with trauma." },
  { q: "How do stress factors affect BMR?", a: "Stress factors raise BMR by 10–100% depending on severity. A mild fever may add 10%, while major surgery or severe burns can double or triple resting energy needs." },
  { q: "Should I adjust my TDEE for stress or illness?", a: "Yes. During illness, injury, or high stress, your body burns more calories at rest. Add 10–20% for minor stress, or 20–50% for major illness or surgery, depending on clinical guidance." },
  { q: "What are common activity multipliers?", a: "Standard TDEE activity multipliers are: sedentary 1.2, lightly active 1.375, moderately active 1.55, very active 1.725, and extra active 1.9." },
  { q: "How do I choose the right activity multiplier?", a: "Be honest about your daily movement. Most people overestimate. If you have a desk job and work out twice a week, start with lightly active (1.375) rather than moderately active." },
];
